WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.595
Listener: What is Sattle doing?

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:05.865
Guide: It connects the shop’s records so
we can follow one order across systems.

00:00:05.865 --> 00:00:08.340
Each dot in this graph is a record, or
node.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.290
This one shows why the refund for order
one oh four is stuck.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:14.590
The refund job called the payment API and
got no reply.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:18.690
In Kubernetes, the owner link tells us
which deployment owns a ReplicaSet.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.290
A network flow names the two ends of a
connection.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:22.665
We call a link a typed edge

00:00:22.665 --> 00:00:25.265
because its type tells us what the
connection means.

00:00:26.495 --> 00:00:28.845
Listener: What ties this reading to that
storage account?

00:00:29.365 --> 00:00:31.615
Guide: They share a full Azure resource
ID.

00:00:31.615 --> 00:00:33.965
Azure Resource Graph names the storage
account,

00:00:33.965 --> 00:00:36.715
and Azure Monitor puts that same ID on its
reading.

00:00:36.715 --> 00:00:38.140
That’s an identity match.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.290
Sattle’s ontology supplies the naming rule

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.565
that ties both records to one account.

00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:44.115
Now follow the DNS name.

00:00:44.115 --> 00:00:47.740
It resolves to an address, and the private
endpoint names the account.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.740
These links connect different things.

00:00:50.953 --> 00:00:53.228
Listener: What happens to that path when
we group the dots?

00:00:53.773 --> 00:00:55.173
Guide: It stays connected.

00:00:55.173 --> 00:00:56.998
Follow the DNS name to the address,

00:00:56.998 --> 00:00:59.498
the private endpoint and the storage
account.

00:00:59.498 --> 00:01:01.473
The dots move into technical groups,

00:01:01.473 --> 00:01:03.698
but the links still cross those
boundaries.

00:01:03.698 --> 00:01:06.773
A projection is a rule for building one
graph from another.

00:01:06.773 --> 00:01:10.373
This one groups records by technology so
you can see how DNS,

00:01:10.373 --> 00:01:12.848
networking and Azure resources fit
together.

00:01:14.078 --> 00:01:15.953
Listener: Where does that leave the refund
job?

00:01:16.498 --> 00:01:17.923
Guide: It belongs to Payout.

00:01:17.923 --> 00:01:21.298
A capability groups software by the job it
does for the shop.

00:01:21.298 --> 00:01:23.873
The job reads a case from Returns Intake.

00:01:23.873 --> 00:01:29.048
Its call to the payment API becomes the
blue arrow from Payout to Payment API.

00:01:29.048 --> 00:01:30.873
That arrow between two groups stands for a

00:01:30.873 --> 00:01:33.298
call you can trace back to the refund job.

00:01:34.495 --> 00:01:37.045
Listener: Which view would help me
investigate the timeout?

00:01:37.565 --> 00:01:38.940
Guide: Start with the dashboard.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.665
It counts successful replies.

00:01:40.665 --> 00:01:43.565
Two out of two payment calls succeeded in
the first minute.

00:01:43.565 --> 00:01:45.890
By the last minute, neither got a reply.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.770
The health tree shows what that means for
Payout.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.170
Its rule needs a successful payment call.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:54.045
Payout is blocked, and the order lookup
still works.

00:01:54.525 --> 00:01:57.150
The Kubernetes view answers a different
question.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.075
Which pods run the refund job?

00:01:59.075 --> 00:02:01.625
Follow the owner links down from the
deployment.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.080
The Azure resource view lists the storage

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.130
account in the shop’s resource group.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:10.935
The DNS and IP view shows how its name
reaches a private address.

00:02:10.935 --> 00:02:13.610
Each view selects what it needs from the
same records.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.760
That’s why you can follow one problem
across all five.

00:02:18.395 --> 00:02:21.020
Listener: Why is my webshop failing, and
when did it start?

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.890
Guide: The first failed database check was
at 10:11 UTC.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.315
Follow the branch from the web service
through the API

00:02:27.315 --> 00:02:29.715
to the database server, pg-shop.

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:32.215
That’s how a database problem reaches the
shop.

00:02:32.215 --> 00:02:34.515
Redis and storage sit on other branches.

00:02:34.515 --> 00:02:37.115
A Health Tree lets you start with the
service people use

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:39.190
and work back to the part that failed.

00:02:40.412 --> 00:02:43.162
Listener: How do we know that’s the
database my shop uses?

00:02:43.707 --> 00:02:45.782
Guide: Look at the name on the Kubernetes
service.

00:02:45.782 --> 00:02:48.057
It points to a PostgreSQL host.

00:02:48.057 --> 00:02:49.907
The structure query builds that host name

00:02:49.907 --> 00:02:51.957
from the Azure server’s name and type.

00:02:51.957 --> 00:02:55.282
The names match, so Sattle connects the
service to that server.

00:02:55.282 --> 00:02:57.282
The health query combines those
connections

00:02:57.282 --> 00:02:59.357
with measurements and signal rules.

00:02:59.357 --> 00:03:02.882
As the source records change, those
connections can change too.

00:03:04.078 --> 00:03:06.278
Listener: How long was the database in
trouble?

00:03:06.823 --> 00:03:08.698
Guide: It failed three checks in a row.

00:03:08.698 --> 00:03:11.748
At 10:11 the database returned its first
zero.

00:03:11.748 --> 00:03:13.998
Sattle raised the alarm at 10:13.

00:03:13.998 --> 00:03:16.248
At 10:14 the server answered again.

00:03:16.248 --> 00:03:18.648
The rule waits for two good minutes in a
row.

00:03:18.648 --> 00:03:21.348
At 10:15 that second good check met the
rule.

00:03:21.348 --> 00:03:24.098
The tree showed the recovery at 10:17.

00:03:25.353 --> 00:03:28.978
Listener: Why does the whole shop show a
problem if only the database failed?

00:03:29.548 --> 00:03:31.373
Guide: Web depends on that database.

00:03:31.373 --> 00:03:33.948
Start at web itself, so its own readings
count.

00:03:33.948 --> 00:03:37.448
Then follow the links called Affects, up
to sixteen steps from web.

00:03:37.448 --> 00:03:41.098
Three links down, pg-shop has severity
three, Unhealthy.

00:03:41.098 --> 00:03:42.823
That outranks the other branches.

00:03:42.823 --> 00:03:45.148
The query keeps the worst severity it
finds,

00:03:45.148 --> 00:03:48.198
which is why the database alarm appears at
the top of the tree.

00:03:49.853 --> 00:03:52.403
Listener: Our API just started using
Redis.

00:03:52.403 --> 00:03:54.328
Will that show up in its health model?

00:03:54.898 --> 00:03:55.398
Guide: Yes.

00:03:55.398 --> 00:03:59.523
Web already uses Redis, and the API
already calls the database.

00:03:59.523 --> 00:04:01.648
At fifteen seconds past ten oh seven,

00:04:01.648 --> 00:04:05.273
Hubble records an API pod sending traffic
to a new address.

00:04:05.273 --> 00:04:08.173
That one call gives Sattle something new
to investigate.

00:04:08.173 --> 00:04:11.573
It can use the records it already has to
work out what’s at the other end.

00:04:12.812 --> 00:04:14.712
Listener: All I see is an IP address.

00:04:14.712 --> 00:04:16.287
How do we know that’s Redis?

00:04:16.832 --> 00:04:18.357
Guide: Look at the two ends of the call.

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:21.807
Kubernetes lists the sending pod under the
API service.

00:04:21.807 --> 00:04:23.732
The destination address matches a private

00:04:23.732 --> 00:04:26.057
endpoint connected to the Redis cache.

00:04:26.057 --> 00:04:29.182
The host name on the call also identifies
that cache.

00:04:29.182 --> 00:04:33.457
Put those matches together and you get a
dependency from the API to Redis.

00:04:34.662 --> 00:04:37.412
Listener: Did someone have to add Redis to
the API’s tree?

00:04:37.982 --> 00:04:38.357
Guide: No.

00:04:38.357 --> 00:04:40.507
Look at the API branch before the call.

00:04:40.507 --> 00:04:42.182
The database is already there.

00:04:42.182 --> 00:04:43.632
Now look after the call.

00:04:43.632 --> 00:04:45.582
Redis appears alongside the database,

00:04:45.582 --> 00:04:48.332
and web still has its own link to the same
cache.

00:04:48.332 --> 00:04:50.932
The existing rules picked up a new
dependency.

00:04:50.932 --> 00:04:53.707
Nobody had to draw that branch or change
the rules.

00:04:54.937 --> 00:04:58.287
Listener: If I find that branch tomorrow,
how can I tell why it’s there?

00:04:58.832 --> 00:05:00.982
Guide: Start with the new link in the
health model.

00:05:00.982 --> 00:05:04.482
It points back to the API’s dependency in
the structure graph.

00:05:04.482 --> 00:05:08.057
One record matches the destination address
to the private endpoint.

00:05:08.057 --> 00:05:10.357
Another matches the host name to Redis.

00:05:10.357 --> 00:05:12.982
Both lead back to the pod’s recorded call.

00:05:12.982 --> 00:05:16.657
That trail from a result to the records
behind it is called provenance.

00:05:18.287 --> 00:05:20.612
Listener: Can we see the query that builds
a health graph?

00:05:21.182 --> 00:05:21.582
Guide: Yes.

00:05:21.582 --> 00:05:25.682
This is a GKQL program from Sattle’s
deployment health experiment.

00:05:25.682 --> 00:05:27.907
The dots in the master graph are pods.

00:05:27.907 --> 00:05:30.257
Web has two, and the database has one.

00:05:30.257 --> 00:05:32.357
The query itself is a graph, too.

00:05:32.357 --> 00:05:34.282
Its boxes are operations.

00:05:34.282 --> 00:05:37.407
A wire tells us which operation uses
another’s result.

00:05:37.407 --> 00:05:40.782
The target graph shows each workload and
the traffic between them.

00:05:41.995 --> 00:05:44.845
Listener: What does the little arrow
inside graph-match look for?

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:47.890
Guide: A ready pod and the deployment that
controls it.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:50.690
The letters p and d hold their places in
the pattern.

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.690
Watch the first web pod light up.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:56.015
In this match, p stands for that pod, and
d stands for web.

00:05:56.015 --> 00:05:57.865
That pairing is called a binding.

00:05:57.865 --> 00:05:59.565
The other web pod fits too.

00:05:59.565 --> 00:06:00.965
So does the database pod.

00:06:00.965 --> 00:06:02.065
Three matches.

00:06:03.287 --> 00:06:05.687
Listener: How do those matches give web a
health state?

00:06:06.207 --> 00:06:08.057
Guide: Start each deployment at zero.

00:06:08.057 --> 00:06:09.832
Add one for every ready pod.

00:06:09.832 --> 00:06:13.407
The summarize step groups those numbers by
deployment and adds them up.

00:06:13.407 --> 00:06:14.482
Web gets two.

00:06:14.482 --> 00:06:15.982
The database gets one.

00:06:15.982 --> 00:06:19.757
The threshold here is zero, so any count
above zero means Healthy.

00:06:19.757 --> 00:06:21.557
Web appears once in the target graph,

00:06:21.557 --> 00:06:23.957
with its deployment ID and a count of two.

00:06:25.187 --> 00:06:29.062
Listener: What happens to the connection
if the database pod stops being ready?

00:06:29.607 --> 00:06:31.332
Guide: Follow the two pod calls.

00:06:31.332 --> 00:06:33.332
Both reach the same database pod,

00:06:33.332 --> 00:06:36.732
so their matches build one Talks edge from
web to the database.

00:06:36.732 --> 00:06:39.607
At ten oh three, the database pod stops
being ready.

00:06:39.607 --> 00:06:41.307
Its count falls to zero.

00:06:41.307 --> 00:06:43.432
The edge takes the worse state of its two
ends,

00:06:43.432 --> 00:06:45.382
so it turns red with the database.

00:06:45.382 --> 00:06:48.182
Web still has two ready pods and stays
green.
